#{[17592186124953 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=$"] [17592186124953 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-2,3)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,3)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {3}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125066 :question/worked-solution "Inhibited growth is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the current population. The capacity is $85$, so the model is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dR}{\\mathrm dt}=k(85-R),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nThe initial population $20$ determines the initial condition $R(0)=20$; it does not change the limiting capacity."] [17592186125220 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$"] [17592186125221 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=$"] [17592186125221 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0}{\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy}-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{xy} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y} & =\\frac{(0-1)(\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}+1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [17592186125222 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=$"] [17592186125222 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{3}+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=-x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186125223 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$"] [17592186125224 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$"] [17592186125224 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\sqrt{4}+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"] [17592186125731 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125754 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we may need to simplify a limit before we can evaluate it. \n\nFor example, let's consider the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2}.\n$$\n\nDirect substitution causes this limit to take the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 00$ because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = \\dfrac{(2)^2 + 2(2)(-1)}{(2)(-1) + 2(-1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling a common factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{y(x+2y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}\\frac{x}{y} &=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,-1)}} \\dfrac{x^2+2xy}{xy+2y^2} = -2.\n$$"] [17592186125809 :tutorial/content "However, it is often easy to show that a limit does not exist for a multivariable function. All we have to do is find two paths on which the limit evaluates to different results. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)} \\dfrac{y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nClearly, direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00.$ Let's compute the limit along the paths $y=x$ and $y=x^2.$ We will see that the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{1+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{y}{x +y} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186125867 :tutorial/content "Consider the multivariable function $f(x,y),$ given by\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ we \"fix\" the variable $y$ (in other words, treat $y$ as if it were a constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{\\color{blue}x}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({\\color{blue}x})+\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (1)+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the term $e^{y}$ vanished when taking the derivative with respect to $x.$ This is because $e^y$ is a function of $y$ only, and since we're treating $y$ as a constant, this entire term is treated as a constant and therefore differentiates to zero (just as a constant would).\n\nSimilarly, to compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ we fix the variable $x$ and then take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{\\color{blue}y}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}(x{{\\color{blue}y}}^{2}+{e}^{{\\color{blue}y}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}({{\\color{blue}y}}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}({e}^{{\\color{blue}y}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2y)+({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy+{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we wish to evaluate a partial derivative at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ we can use any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f(x_0,y_0)} {\\partial x} =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\\Bigg|_{(x_0,y_0)} = f_x(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nThe notations for $f_y(x_0,y_0)$ follow the same patterns."] [17592186144137 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144142] [17592186144143 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144147] [17592186144292 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144297] [17592186144298 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144300] [17592186144309 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144313] [17592186144312 :answer/value "y^{2} - e^{xy} + 2x = 0"] [17592186144315 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144316] [17592186146462 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186146467] [17592186147391 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$\n\nFor real solutions, require $x\\ne0$ and $2x^5+C>0$. The implicit relation retains both signs $y=\\pm\\sqrt{(2x^5+C)/(5x^2)}$. The original equation is undefined at $y=0$."] [17592186147725 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$"] [17592186147725 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse intervals where $C-e^{2x}\\ne0$. The family displayed in the choices is nonzero. The original equation also admits $y=0$, which was excluded when dividing by a power of $y$."] [17592186147726 :question/problem "Find the general **nonzero real** solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=y^4-xy.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval with $x\\ne0$ where the solution is finite. Interpret cube roots as real cube roots."] [17592186147726 :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, write the equation in Bernoulli form:\n\n$$\ny'+\\frac1x y=\\frac1{x^2}y^4.\n$$\nWe seek the nonzero real solutions, so divide by $y^4$ and set $z=y^{-3}$. The chain rule gives $z'=-3y^{-4}y'$, hence\n\n$$\nz'-\\frac3x z=-\\frac3{x^2}.\n$$\nOn any interval avoiding $x=0$, an integrating factor is $I(x)=x^{-3}$, because $I'/I=-3/x$. Multiplying and integrating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{-3}z)'&=-3x^{-5},\\\\\nx^{-3}z&=\\frac3{4x^4}+K,\\\\\nz&=\\frac3{4x}+Kx^3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow substitute $z=y^{-3}$ and absorb the factor $4$ into the arbitrary constant, writing $C=4K$:\n\n$$\ny^3=\\frac{4x}{3+Cx^4},\\qquad\n\\boxed{y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4x}{3+Cx^4}}}.\n$$\nThe cube root is the **real** cube root, so its argument may be negative. Use intervals with $x\\ne0$ and $3+Cx^4\\ne0$; on these intervals the family is real, finite and nonzero. The invertible substitution $z=y^{-3}$ recovers every nonzero solution there.\n\nTo verify the result without imposing a positive branch, put $g(x)=4x/(3+Cx^4)$. Since $y^3=g(x)\\ne0$, implicit differentiation gives $y'=yg'/(3g)$, and\n\n$$\nx^2y'-y^4+xy\n=y\\left(\\frac{x^2g'}{3g}-g+x\\right)=0.\n$$\nThe original equation also admits the equilibrium solution $y=0$. It is excluded only by the question's explicit **nonzero** condition; it was lost when dividing by $y^4$ and must be checked separately."] [17592186147729 :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli equation has the form $y'+P(x)y=Q(x)y^n$ with $n\\ne0,1$.\n\nI has this form with $n=2$. II has a $y^2$ term instead of the required linear $P(x)y$ term. III has this form with $n=-1/2$, on its real domain $y>0$.\n\nTherefore the correct selection is **I and III only**."] [17592186147730 :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli equation has the form $y'+P(x)y=Q(x)y^n$ with $n\\ne0,1$.\n\nFor I, division by $y$ gives $y'+x^2y^2=e^xy^3$, which still has two nonlinear powers. For II, division by $y^2$ gives\n$$\ny'+x^2y=e^xy^2,\n$$\nwhich is Bernoulli with $n=2$ on intervals where $y\\ne0$. The original II also admits $y=0$, which should be checked separately when solving. III contains trigonometric functions of $y$ and does not have Bernoulli form.\n\nTherefore the correct selection is **II only**."]}
